const SPEC_DATA = [
// Consumer Protection 2019 (additional sections)
["e","Consumer — electronic service provider liability","Section 94 CPA 2019: Mediation Cell attached to each commission. S.81: product liability — product service provider liable for defective service, insufficient instructions, failure to issue warning. S.82: product liability of product seller — liable if product seller knows of defect; alters product; exercises control over product character; makes express warranty. S.83: product manufacturer liability — design defect, manufacturing defect, inadequate instructions, deviation from specifications.","7","Product Liability","Consumer Law",["CPA product liability product seller manufacturer service provider S81 S82 S83 design defect instruction warranty 2019"]],
["f","Consumer — Misleading advertisement — Endorser liability","S.21 CPA 2019: endorser (celebrity) who endorses a misleading advertisement shall be liable if they endorsed the advertisement with due diligence and without knowledge that the claim is false, they shall not be liable for first offence. On second violation, endorser may be prohibited from making endorsement for up to 1 year; third violation up to 3 years. Endorser means any person who is associated with an advertisement.","7","Offences","Consumer Law",["CPA misleading advertisement endorser celebrity liability section 21 due diligence one year three years prohibition false claim 2019"]],
["g","Consumer — E-commerce regulations","Consumer Protection (E-Commerce) Rules 2020: every e-commerce entity shall display their country of origin on product, seller details, complaint mechanism, no unfair practices in returns/cancellation. Flash sales that prevent consumers from purchasing — prohibited. Marketplace entities must ensure sellers register and comply. Grievance officer to be appointed by every e-commerce entity.","8","E-Commerce","Consumer Law",["CPA e-commerce rules 2020 country of origin seller details complaint flash sales marketplace grievance officer unfair practices consumer"]],
// RTI Act 2005 (additional sections)
["RTI3","RTI — Third party information","S.11 RTI Act: where information relates to third party and has been treated as confidential, CPIO shall give notice to third party of request; third party may within ten days make representation against disclosure. Notwithstanding, if CPIO decides to disclose, third party may appeal. Third party information may be disclosed if public interest in disclosure outweighs harm. Trade secrets and commercial confidence: exempt unless larger public interest.","2","RTI","Right to Information",["RTI third party confidential S11 notice ten days representation trade secret commercial confidence public interest disclosure"]],
["RTI4","RTI — Information Commission composition","S.12 RTI: Central Information Commission — Chief Information Commissioner + up to 10 Information Commissioners, appointed by President on recommendation of committee (PM + Leader of Opposition + Cabinet Minister nominated by PM). S.15: State Information Commission — SCIC + up to 10 SICs, appointed by Governor on recommendation of committee.","4","RTI","Right to Information",["RTI Central Information Commission section 12 CIC ten commissioners President Prime Minister Leader of Opposition State SIC Governor"]],
["RTI5","RTI — Deemed public authorities and exempted organisations","S.2(h) RTI: public authority includes bodies owned/controlled/substantially financed by government; NGOs substantially financed. S.24: Intelligence Bureau, Research and Analysis Wing (RAW) and certain security organisations in Second Schedule are exempt from RTI. SC in DDA case: political parties are not public authorities under RTI (controversial — Law Commission recommended to include).","2","RTI","Right to Information",["RTI public authority section 2(h) NGO financed exempt IB RAW Second Schedule political parties not covered SC DDA"]],
// Motor Vehicles Act 1988 (additional sections)
["MVA_ins","Motor Vehicles — Third Party Insurance — compulsory","S.146 MVA: No person shall use or allow to use a motor vehicle in a public place unless there is in force in relation to that vehicle a policy of insurance complying with the requirements of this Chapter (Chapter XI). S.147: policy must cover third party liability, liability under Workmen's Compensation Act 1923, liability of passengers. The Solatium Fund (S.163) provides compensation even where no insurance exists.","11","Insurance","Motor Vehicles Law",["MVA third party insurance compulsory S146 S147 S163 Solatium Fund public place third party liability passengers"]],
["MVA_hit","Motor Vehicles — Hit and run compensation","S.161A MVA (2019 Amendment): Hit and Run Motor Accident Scheme — central government provides compensation for death (Rs 2 lakh) and grievous hurt (Rs 50,000) in hit and run accidents. No need to identify the vehicle. The Solatium Scheme is administered through GIC. A new scheme (2022) revised to Rs 2 lakh for death.","11","Insurance","Motor Vehicles Law",["MVA hit and run compensation scheme Rs 2 lakh Rs 50000 S161A 2019 amendment GIC Solatium Fund death grievous hurt identify vehicle"]],
["MVA_comp","Motor Vehicles — Compensation for road accident — MACT","S.166 MVA: application to Motor Accident Claims Tribunal for compensation for death/permanent injury. S.168: award of compensation — just compensation. S.163A: structured formula for compensation (without fault liability for low income victims). Sarla Verma formula (2009 SC): future prospects added to salary for calculating compensation in fatal accidents. National Insurance v. Pranay Sethi (2017): 40% addition to salary for future prospects.","11","Compensation","Motor Vehicles Law",["MVA MACT compensation S166 S168 S163A just compensation Sarla Verma formula 2009 fatal accident future prospects 40 percent Pranay Sethi 2017"]],
["MVA_dl","Motor Vehicles — Driving Licence — 2019 amendments","S.3 MVA: No person shall drive a motor vehicle in a public place unless he holds an effective driving licence. 2019 Amendment: community service for minor violations; e-Challan system; no arrest for minor violations; deemed licence if not renewed within 1 year; National Register of Driving Licences (NRDL); learner's licence online; testing at private facilities (Accredited Driving Training Centres).","2","Licensing","Motor Vehicles Law",["MVA driving licence S3 2019 e-challan community service minor violations NRDL online learner testing private ADTC no arrest"]],
["MVA_pen","Motor Vehicles — Enhanced penalties 2019","2019 Amendment substantially increased penalties: drunken driving — Rs 10,000 (first) / Rs 15,000 (second); racing — Rs 5,000; over-speeding — Rs 1,000 to 2,000 (LMV) / Rs 4,000 (medium/heavy vehicle); not wearing helmet — Rs 1,000 + 3 months suspension; not wearing seatbelt — Rs 1,000; dangerous driving — Rs 1,000 to 5,000; juvenile driving — Rs 25,000 + guardian 3 years imprisonment + registration cancelled.","15","Penalties","Motor Vehicles Law",["MVA 2019 enhanced penalties drunken driving Rs 10000 15000 helmet Rs 1000 seatbelt juvenile Rs 25000 guardian 3 years registration cancelled over-speeding racing"]],
// Labour Code on Wages (extended)
["COW_min","Code on Wages — Minimum wage revision mechanism","S.8 COW: The appropriate Government shall review and revise minimum wages at intervals not exceeding five years. Factors for fixation: cost of living, nature of work, area. S.9: number of hours of normal working day (8 hours per day for scheduled employments). Floor wage: Central Government notifies national floor wage — no State can fix below floor wage. The floor wage concept ensures uniformity across India.","2","Minimum Wages","Labour Law / Code on Wages",["Code on Wages minimum wage five years review revise S8 S9 floor wage Central Government uniform national 8 hours working day"]],
["COW_deduct","Code on Wages — Deductions from wages","S.18 COW: deductions from wages of an employee may be made only in the following cases: fines (not exceeding 3% of wages in a month); absence from duty; damage/loss of goods; house accommodation; medical facilities; recovery of advances; employee's contribution to PF/ESI; income tax; loan by employer; subscription for cooperative society. Deductions not to exceed 50% (75% with PF) of total wages.","3","Payment of Wages","Labour Law / Code on Wages",["Code on Wages deductions S18 fines 3 percent absence damage house accommodation PF ESI income tax loan 50 percent 75 percent"]],
// Banking (extended)
["BRA_NPA","Banking — Non-Performing Assets (NPA)","An asset (loan/advance) becomes NPA when: (a) interest/principal remains overdue for more than 90 days (for term loans); (b) the account remains out of order for 90 days (for cash credit/overdraft); (c) the bill remains overdue for 90 days (for bills). NPA classification: Substandard (upto 12 months NPA), Doubtful (12-36 months), Loss (beyond 36 months or identified as unrecoverable). RBI's SARFAESI Act 2002 allows banks to recover without court for secured assets.","5","Banking Regulation","Commercial Law / Banking",["NPA non-performing asset 90 days term loan overdue substandard doubtful loss SARFAESI 2002 RBI bank secured asset recover without court"]],
["SARFAESI","SARFAESI Act 2002 — Section 13 — Enforcement of security interest","S.13 SARFAESI: Secured creditor may enforce security interest without court's intervention on borrower's default. Bank gives 60-day notice to borrower to pay. If not paid: bank may take possession of secured assets; take over management of the business; appoint manager to manage assets; require debtors of borrower to pay. Borrower may represent within 30 days. Appeal to DRT (Debt Recovery Tribunal) under S.17.","3","Enforcement","Commercial Law / Banking",["SARFAESI S13 secured creditor 60-day notice possession management borrower DRT appeal 30 days default enforce without court"]],
["DRT","Debt Recovery Tribunals — RDDBFI Act 1993","Recovery of Debts Due to Banks and Financial Institutions Act 1993: DRT established for banks to recover debts above Rs 20 lakh (originally Rs 10 lakh). Banks/FIs file application; DRT hears and passes Recovery Certificate. Appeal to DRAT (Appellate Tribunal) on pre-deposit of 75% of award. Special provision: DRT has power to attach before judgment, examine parties under oath. No civil court has jurisdiction once DRT is approached.","2","Recovery","Commercial Law / Banking",["DRT RDDBFI Act 1993 Rs 20 lakh bank financial institution Recovery Certificate DRAT 75 percent civil court no jurisdiction"]],
// GST (extended)
["GST_comp","GST — Composition scheme — S.10","S.10 CGST: Registered persons with aggregate turnover not exceeding Rs 1.5 crore (Rs 75 lakh for special category States) may opt for composition scheme. Rates: manufacturers — 1% of turnover; traders — 1%; restaurants (not serving alcohol) — 5%. Cannot issue tax invoice; no input tax credit; restricted to supplies within State. Composition dealer cannot supply inter-State. Filing: quarterly returns and annual returns.","4","Levy","Tax Law / GST",["GST composition scheme S10 1.5 crore 75 lakh quarterly 1 percent 5 percent no ITC no interstate invoice annual return"]],
["GST_rcm","GST — Reverse charge mechanism — S.9(3)","Under S.9(3) CGST Act, Government notified certain goods and services where reverse charge applies — recipient pays GST instead of supplier. Examples: legal services by advocates to body corporate; services by GTA (goods transport agency); import of services; director remuneration from company; services by insurance agent; sponsorship services. Under reverse charge, no input tax credit to supplier.","3","Levy","Tax Law / GST",["GST reverse charge mechanism S9(3) recipient pays advocate GTA import services director insurance agent sponsorship no ITC supplier"]],
["GST_ewaybill","GST — E-way bill","E-way bill is an electronic document generated for movement of goods exceeding Rs 50,000 in value. Required for: inter-State movement; intra-State movement (State specific rules). Validity: 100 km/day (max: EWB valid for distance covered). Penalty for movement without EWB: Rs 10,000 or tax evaded, whichever is higher. Physical verification by tax officers at checkpost or during transit.","8","Returns","Tax Law / GST",["GST e-way bill Rs 50000 inter-state intra-state 100 km validity penalty Rs 10000 tax evaded physical verification checkpost transit"]],
["GST_refund","GST — Refund of tax — S.54","Application for refund to be filed within 2 years from relevant date. Relevant date: for exports — date of export; for excess payment — date of payment; for input tax credit — end of financial year. Refund in case of: export of goods/services; inverted duty structure; supplies to SEZ; deemed exports; excess tax paid. Provisional refund of 90% in export cases. Refund not to be withheld except on grounds of tax due.","13","Refunds","Tax Law / GST",["GST refund S54 two years export ITC inverted duty SEZ 90 percent provisional excess tax paid relevant date"]],
// Income Tax (extended)
["IT_TDS","Income Tax — TDS — Sections 192-206AA","Tax Deducted at Source (TDS): employer deducts TDS from salary (S.192); bank deducts from interest (S.194A — 10%); contractor payments (S.194C — 1% individual/HUF, 2% others); professional fees (S.194J — 10%); rent (S.194I — 10% for P&M, 10% for land/building). PAN not furnished: TDS at higher of 20% or applicable rate (S.206AA). Non-deduction of TDS: disallowance of 30% of expenditure (S.40(a)(ia)).","15","TDS","Tax Law / Income Tax",["Income Tax TDS S192 salary S194A interest S194C contractor S194J professional S194I rent 10 percent S206AA PAN 20 percent 30 percent disallow S40"]],
["IT_advance","Income Tax — Advance tax — Sections 207-211","Every assessee whose estimated tax liability for the year is Rs 10,000 or more shall pay advance tax in instalments. For individuals (non-business): 2 instalments (December: 75%, March: 100%). For businesses: 4 instalments (June: 15%, September: 45%, December: 75%, March: 100%). Senior citizens not having business income: exempted from advance tax. Interest under S.234B for non-payment, S.234C for deferment.","16","Advance Tax","Tax Law / Income Tax",["Income Tax advance tax S207 Rs 10000 instalments June September December March 15 45 75 100 percent senior citizen exempted S234B S234C interest"]],
["IT_ded","Income Tax — Common deductions under Chapter VI-A","S.80C: Rs 1.5 lakh (LIC, PPF, ELSS, NSC, housing loan principal, tuition fees). S.80D: Medical insurance premium — Rs 25,000 (self/family), Rs 50,000 (senior citizens). S.80E: Education loan interest — entire interest for 8 years. S.80G: Donations — 50% or 100% with/without limit. S.80TTA: Interest from savings account up to Rs 10,000. S.80U: Disability — Rs 75,000 (disability), Rs 1.25 lakh (severe disability). New tax regime (S.115BAC) — most deductions not available.","6A","Deductions","Tax Law / Income Tax",["Income Tax deductions 80C 80D 80E 80G 80TTA 80U 1.5 lakh Rs 25000 Rs 50000 donation disability education loan medical new regime 115BAC"]],
// Company Law (extended)
["CA_winding","Companies Act 2013 — Winding up by NCLT (Tribunal)","S.272 CA 2013: petition for winding up by Tribunal may be filed by: company; any creditor; any contributory; any RD/OL authorised by CG. S.271: grounds — unable to pay debts; just and equitable; default in filing financial statements for 5 consecutive years; acting against State security/integrity; fraud; illegal objects; number of members below legal minimum. NCLT appoints liquidator. After winding up order, all suits against company stay.","20","Winding Up","Company Law",["CA2013 winding up NCLT Tribunal petition S272 S271 unable to pay debts just equitable fraud illegal stay suits liquidator"]],
["CA_compromise","Companies Act 2013 — Mergers and Compromise — S.230","S.230 CA 2013: Where compromise or arrangement proposed between company and members/creditors, company/liquidator/creditor may apply to NCLT. NCLT orders meeting. If 3/4 in value (majority present and voting) agree, NCLT may sanction. Merger without NCLT: fast-track merger (S.233) — between holding and wholly owned subsidiary or between two small companies — no NCLT needed, only Central Government approval.","15","Compromises","Company Law",["CA2013 compromise arrangement S230 NCLT meeting 3/4 majority sanction fast-track merger S233 holding subsidiary small companies Central Government"]],
["CA_NCLT","National Company Law Tribunal — Jurisdiction","NCLT established under S.408 CA 2013. Jurisdiction: company law matters, insolvency, winding up, mergers, oppression and mismanagement. NCLAT: appellate tribunal from NCLT. Appeal from NCLAT to Supreme Court on question of law. NCLT replaced Company Law Board (CLB), Board for Industrial and Financial Reconstruction (BIFR), and Appellate Authority for Industrial and Financial Reconstruction (AAIFR). Quorum: two judicial + one technical member.","22","Tribunals","Company Law",["CA2013 NCLT S408 jurisdiction winding up insolvency merger oppression NCLAT Supreme Court replaced CLB BIFR AAIFR"]],
// SEBI (extended)
["SEBI_insider","SEBI — Insider Trading — Prohibition of Insider Trading Regulations 2015","SEBI (PIT) Regulations 2015: 'unpublished price sensitive information' (UPSI) means information relating to a company or its securities that is not generally available and likely to materially affect price. Prohibition: no insider (person connected with company or having UPSI) shall trade while in possession of UPSI. Trading Window: companies must restrict insider trading through trading window closure around declaration of results, dividends, M&A announcements. Structured Digital Database (SDD) to be maintained.","3","Insider Trading","Commercial Law / Securities",["SEBI insider trading UPSI 2015 regulations trading window closure price sensitive material non-public SDD structured digital database prohibition connected person"]],
["SEBI_takeover","SEBI — Takeover Code — SEBI (SAST) Regulations 2011","Substantial Acquisition of Shares and Takeovers (SAST) Regulations 2011: Open offer triggered when: (a) acquirer crosses 25% (threshold) from outside the target; (b) creeping acquisition — more than 5% in a year when holding is between 25-75%; (c) acquirer acquires control. Open offer: minimum 26% of voting rights from public shareholders at offer price. Offer price: higher of volume-weighted average price over 60 weeks or 26 weeks.","3","Takeovers","Commercial Law / Securities",["SEBI takeover SAST 2011 open offer 25 percent threshold creeping 5 percent 26 percent public shareholders offer price 60 weeks 26 weeks VWAP"]],
["SEBI_mf","SEBI — Mutual Funds Regulations 1996","SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations 1996: Mutual fund is a trust that pools savings of investors and invests in various securities. Sponsor, trustee, AMC (Asset Management Company) structure. Types: equity, debt, hybrid, index, ETF. Categories (SEBI 2017 Circular): large cap, mid cap, small cap, multi-cap. NAV (Net Asset Value): per unit value = total assets - liabilities / units. Exit load: charges on redemption before specified period. AMFI (Association of Mutual Funds in India) — self-regulatory body.","3","Mutual Funds","Commercial Law / Securities",["SEBI mutual funds AMC trustee sponsor NAV exit load AMFI equity debt hybrid index ETF large cap mid cap small cap 2017 circular"]],
// RERA (extended)
["RERA_allottee","RERA — Rights of allottees — S.19","S.19 RERA: Every allottee shall have the right to: obtain information relating to sanctioned plans, layout plans and specifications as approved by the competent authority; know stage-wise time schedule of completion; claim possession of apartment/plot per terms of agreement; claim refund with interest (at prescribed rate) if promoter fails to complete or deliver possession; have necessary documents executed after possession; after obtaining possession, participate in formation of association.","4","Rights of Allottees","Property Law / RERA",["RERA allottee rights S19 sanctioned plans time schedule completion claim possession refund interest association formation documents after possession"]],
["RERA_agent","RERA — Obligations of real estate agents — S.9-10","S.9 RERA: Every real estate agent must register with RERA Authority before facilitating sale of any plot/apartment/building. S.10: Obligations — maintain books of accounts; not facilitate transactions where project not registered; not indulge in unfair trade practices; facilitate redressal of disputes; maintain proper records. Penalty for violation: Rs 10,000 per day (max Rs 5 lakh) and cancellation of registration.","3","Registration","Property Law / RERA",["RERA real estate agent S9 S10 register maintain books no unregistered project unfair practices records Rs 10000 per day Rs 5 lakh cancellation"]],
];
SPEC_DATA.forEach(([sec,title,desc,ch,cht,cat,kw])=>{
  const actName = cat.includes("Consumer")?"Consumer Protection Act, 2019":
    cat.includes("Information")?"Right to Information Act, 2005":
    cat.includes("Motor")?"Motor Vehicles Act, 1988":
    cat.includes("Wages")?"Code on Wages, 2019":
    cat.includes("Banking")?"Banking Regulation Act, 1949":
    cat.includes("GST")?"Goods and Services Tax Act, 2017":
    cat.includes("Income Tax")?"Income Tax Act, 1961":
    cat.includes("Company")?"Companies Act, 2013":
    cat.includes("Securities")?"Securities and Exchange Board of India Act, 1992":
    cat.includes("RERA")?"Real Estate (Regulation and Development) Act, 2016":"Special Acts";
  const shortName = cat.includes("Consumer")?"CPA":
    cat.includes("Information")?"RTI":
    cat.includes("Motor")?"MVA":
    cat.includes("Wages")?"COW":
    cat.includes("Banking")?"BRA/SARFAESI/DRT":
    cat.includes("GST")?"GST":
    cat.includes("Income Tax")?"ITA61":
    cat.includes("Company")?"CA2013":
    cat.includes("Securities")?"SEBI":
    cat.includes("RERA")?"RERA":"SPECIAL";
  special.push(e(actName,shortName,sec,title,desc,ch,cht,cat,kw));
});
save('bulk_special_acts.json', special);

/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   FILE 3 — bulk_personal_family_laws.json
   HMA, HSA, Muslim, Christian, Parsi, Succession
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
const family = [];
const FAM_DATA = [
// Hindu Marriage Act full remaining sections
["HMA_29A","HMA — Divorce by mutual consent — procedure details","S.13B HMA: First motion — both parties present petition jointly. Second motion — after 6 months (not more than 18 months from first motion). If either party withdraws: petition dismissed. SC in Sureshta Devi (1991): 6-month period is mandatory, cannot be waived. But SC in Amardeep Singh (2017): 6-month period can be waived by court exercising jurisdiction under Article 142 if marriage irretrievably broken down. Under BNS 2023, dissolution by mutual consent provisions retained with 1 year separation requirement.","HMA-4","Divorce","Family Law / Hindu",["HMA mutual consent S13B 6 months 18 months waive Sureshta Devi Amardeep Singh Article 142 irretrievably broken first second motion"]],
["HMA_irretrievable","HMA — Irretrievable breakdown of marriage","Law Commission Report 71st (1978) and 202nd (2009) recommended adding irretrievable breakdown as a ground for divorce under HMA. SC has used Article 142 to grant divorce on ground of irretrievable breakdown (Anjana Kishore 2002, Shilpa Sailesh 2023). In Shilpa Sailesh v. Varun Sreenivasan (2023), Constitution Bench held: SC can use Article 142 to dissolve marriage on irretrievable breakdown; waive Section 13B(2) waiting period; grant divorce in exercise of its constitutional jurisdiction even if only one party wants divorce.","HMA-4","Divorce","Family Law / Hindu",["HMA irretrievable breakdown divorce ground Article 142 Shilpa Sailesh 2023 Constitution Bench Anjana Kishore Law Commission waive 13B"]],
["HMA_alimony","HMA — Calculation of permanent alimony and maintenance","S.25 HMA: Factors for permanent alimony: income and property of respondent; income and property of applicant; conduct of parties; other circumstances. SC in Vinny Parmar (2011): wife not entitled to alimony if she has sufficient income. SC in Rajnesh v. Neha (2020): uniform guidelines — disclosure of assets mandatory; criteria for maintenance; retroactivity of maintenance orders; maintenance covers food, clothing, residence, education. AIMPLB and Muslim personal law have separate maintenance rules.","HMA-5","Maintenance","Family Law / Hindu",["HMA permanent alimony S25 income property conduct circumstances Vinny Parmar wife sufficient Rajnesh Neha 2020 disclosure assets retroactive uniform guidelines"]],
// Hindu Succession Act additional
["HSA_2005","HSA — 2005 Amendment — daughter as coparcener","Hindu Succession (Amendment) Act 2005: S.6 amended — daughter of coparcener becomes coparcener by birth in Mitakshara Joint Hindu Family, has same rights and liabilities as son. SC in Prakash v. Phulavati (2015): amendment applies only if father (coparcener) was alive on 9 September 2005. SC in Vineeta Sharma v. Rakesh Sharma (2020): overruled Phulavati — amendment is retroactive, daughter gets rights irrespective of whether father was alive on 9.9.2005; it is a right by birth. Equal right to partition and ancestral property.","HSA-2","Coparcenary","Family Law / Hindu",["HSA 2005 amendment daughter coparcener section 6 Mitakshara Prakash Phulavati 2015 Vineeta Sharma 2020 retroactive birth right partition ancestral property"]],
["HSA_will","HSA — Testamentary succession — Wills under Hindu law","S.30 HSA: A Hindu may dispose by will of: (a) his/her separate property; (b) his/her undivided interest in Joint Hindu Family (Mitakshara) property — he can bequeath his interest to outsiders, not bound to give to coparceners. Under Dayabhaga school (Bengal/Assam), all property can be bequeathed. Will must comply with Indian Succession Act 1925 — two witnesses required. Holograph will: entirely in handwriting of testator, needs witnesses. Nuncupative will (oral): valid in certain emergency conditions.","HSA-4","Testamentary","Family Law / Hindu",["HSA will S30 separate property undivided interest Mitakshara bequeath ISA 1925 two witnesses holograph nuncupative oral Dayabhaga Bengal Assam"]],
// Muslim Law detailed
["Muslim_talaq_types","Muslim Law — Types of Talaq","Talaq-e-Ahsan (best form): one revocable pronouncement in tuhr (purity period) followed by iddat of 3 months; can be revoked. Talaq-e-Hasan (good form): three pronouncements in three successive tuhrs; irrevocable after third. Talaq-e-Biddat (bad form/triple talaq): three simultaneous pronouncements — irrevocable, sinful. Triple talaq declared void and criminal by Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Marriage) Act 2019 after SC decision in Shayara Bano (2017). Talaq by husband only; wife can seek khul (divorce with return of dower) or invoke mubarat (mutual divorce).","1","Dissolution","Family Law / Muslim",["Muslim talaq types Ahsan Hasan Biddat triple talaq irrevocable tuhr iddat three months revocable Shayara Bano 2017 void criminal 2019 khul mubarat"]],
["Muslim_mehr","Muslim Law — Mehr/Dower","Mehr (dower) is a sum of money or other property which the wife is entitled to receive from husband in consideration of marriage. Types: Prompt mehr (mahr-e-muajjal — payable immediately on demand) and deferred mehr (mahr-e-muwajjal — payable on dissolution of marriage or death). Minimum mehr: 10 dirhams (there is no maximum). Mehr is a debt on husband — it is recoverable by wife as unsecured creditor. Wife can refuse cohabitation until prompt mehr is paid. Mehr survives divorce and death of husband.","2","Marriage Rights","Family Law / Muslim",["Muslim mehr dower prompt deferred mahr muajjal muwajjal debt husband minimum maximum cohabitation refuse divorce death recoverable"]],
["Muslim_divorce_wife","Muslim Law — Divorce by wife — Khul, Mubarat, Fasakh","Khul: wife asks for divorce in return for returning dower/compensation; husband agrees. Mubarat: mutual divorce by consent; both agree to dissolve; no compensation needed. Fasakh: dissolution of marriage through court — grounds under Dissolution of Muslim Marriages Act 1939 (S.2): husband missing 4 years; failure to maintain 2 years; imprisonment 7 years+; failure to perform marital obligations 3 years; impotency; insanity 2 years; cruelty; husband suffering venereal disease; repudiation at option of puberty (Khyar-ul-Bulugh).","3","Dissolution","Family Law / Muslim",["Muslim divorce wife Khul Mubarat Fasakh DMMA 1939 S2 missing 4 years maintenance imprisonment 7 years impotency insanity cruelty venereal disease puberty"]],
["Muslim_inheritance","Muslim Law — Inheritance — Sunni and Shia differences","Sunni law (majority): Quran provides for fixed shares (Quranic heirs) — daughter gets half of son's share; widow 1/4 or 1/8; mother 1/6 or 1/3. Rules of Aul (abatement) and Radd (return). Shia law (minority — mainly Khojas, Bohras): different rules — no right of representation; descendants through females inherit; daughter excludes collaterals. Common: sons exclude daughters in residue; murder bars inheritance; adoption not recognised. Will: limited to 1/3 of estate.","4","Succession","Family Law / Muslim",["Muslim inheritance Sunni Shia Quranic heirs fixed shares daughter half son widow Aul Radd will 1/3 adoption murder bar Khojas Bohras difference"]],
["Muslim_waqf","Muslim Law — Waqf","Waqf means permanent dedication of property for pious or charitable purposes. Types: waqf-ala-l-awlad (for family descendants — family waqf), waqf-al-masajid (for mosque). Requirements: permanent dedication; irrevocable; for pious/charitable purpose. Waqf Act 1995: Central Waqf Council; State Waqf Board; Waqf Tribunal (for disputes); powers of Survey Commissioner; powers of Mutawalli. 2024 Waqf Amendment Act (controversial) — proposed changes to Waqf Board composition and powers.","5","Waqf","Family Law / Muslim",["Muslim waqf permanent dedication family mosque Waqf Act 1995 Central Council State Board Tribunal Mutawalli Survey Commissioner 2024 amendment"]],
// Christian Marriage law
["CMA_marriage","Indian Christian Marriage Act 1872 — Marriage conditions","S.4 ICMA: Every marriage between persons both of whom are Christians shall be solemnized in accordance with the Act. S.60: Marriage solemnized by: Minister of Religion; Registrar of Christian Marriages; Marriage Registrar appointed by State Government; Person licensed by State Government. S.61: Conditions — neither party is married, parties not within prohibited degrees, bridegroom not under 21 and bride not under 18, publication of notice 14 days before.","1","Marriage","Family Law / Christian",["ICMA Christian marriage conditions S4 S60 S61 Minister Registrar licensed State Government prohibited degrees 21 18 years notice 14 days"]],
["CMA_divorce","Divorce under Christian law — DMCA 1869","Divorce Act 1869 (applies to Christians): grounds for divorce — adultery; cruelty; desertion for 2 years+; conversion; unsound mind; venereal disease; leprosy; presumed death. S.10 Divorce Act: either party may petition. S.7 Divorce Act: court exercising divorce jurisdiction — District Court. S.10A (2001 Amendment): mutual consent divorce — similar to HMA S.13B. Christian women can now seek divorce on same grounds as husband (gender equality amendment). Alimony and maintenance: S.36, S.37 Divorce Act.","1","Divorce","Family Law / Christian",["Divorce Act 1869 Christian grounds adultery cruelty desertion 2 years conversion insanity venereal leprosy presumed death mutual consent 2001 amendment District Court S10A"]],
// Indian Succession Act extended
["ISA_will_types","Indian Succession Act 1925 — Privileged and unprivileged wills","Unprivileged will (S.63): signed by testator; attested by 2 witnesses present at same time; each witness signs in presence of testator. Privileged will (S.65): soldier/airman employed in expedition or warfare, or mariner at sea — may make will without formal requirements (oral or written without witnesses). Holograph will — entirely in testator's handwriting — treated as privileged. Nuncupative will — oral — valid only for privileged persons. Codicil — supplementary document changing a will.","9","Wills","Family Law / Succession",["ISA will unprivileged privileged soldier airman seaman expedition oral holograph codicil two witnesses signed attested S63 S65"]],
["ISA_executor","Indian Succession Act — Executor and Administrator","Executor: person appointed by testator to carry out will. Administrator: person appointed by court when there is no executor or executor refuses. Probate: court certificate confirming will is genuine. Letters of Administration: granted when no will. S.212 ISA: no right to act as executor until probate/letters of admin granted. S.222: only executor can get probate; personal representative steps into shoes of deceased.","10","Probate","Family Law / Succession",["ISA executor administrator probate letters of administration S212 S222 personal representative testator court certificate genuine will deceased rights"]],
["ISA_intestate","Indian Succession Act — Intestate succession for non-Hindus","Part V ISA: Rules for Christians, Parsis, Jews dying intestate. S.32: Property devolves on: lineal descendants; widow/widower; kindred; government (if no kindred). S.34: widow/widower and one lineal descendant — each gets 1/2. Two or more lineal descendants — widow/widower 1/3, rest 2/3 among descendants. Parsis: S.51-56 ISA — own rules. S.42: half-blood heirs succeed equally with full-blood at same degree.","11","Intestate Succession","Family Law / Succession",["ISA intestate Christians Parsis Jews S32 lineal descendants widow widower 1/2 1/3 government kindred S42 half blood full blood equal Parsis S51"]],
// Guardians and Wards Act extended
["GWA_welfare","GWA — Best interest / welfare of minor — paramount consideration","S.17 GWA: In considering what will be for the welfare of the minor, the court shall have regard to the age, sex, religion of the minor; character and capacity of proposed guardian; nearness of kin; wishes of deceased parent; existing relations of guardian with minor or property. SC in Mausami Moitra Ganguli (2008): welfare of child is paramount — not the rights of parents. 'Welfare' includes material well-being, moral, psychological and emotional well-being, education. Child's preference above 9-10 years given weight.","2","Appointment","Family Law / Guardianship",["GWA best interest minor welfare S17 age sex religion kin wishes character paramount Mausami Moitra material moral psychological education child preference"]],
["GWA_custody","GWA — Custody battles — Joint custody, Visitation rights","Indian law does not expressly provide for joint custody but courts increasingly award it. SC in Yashita Sahu (2020): joint custody can be awarded even in contested cases. Visitation rights: non-custodial parent has right to meet/interact with child. Court may appoint amicus curiae or child psychologist to assess child's preference. Passport for child: both parents' consent required or court order. Foreign custody orders: not automatically enforceable in India — courts apply welfare test.","2","Custody","Family Law / Guardianship",["GWA custody joint custody visitation rights Yashita Sahu 2020 SC passport consent foreign order welfare test child psychologist amicus curiae"]],
// Juvenile Justice Act extended
["JJA_CWC","JJA — Child Welfare Committee — Functions","S.27 JJA: Every district shall have a Child Welfare Committee (CWC) consisting of a chairperson and 4 other members (at least one woman, one expert in child welfare). Functions: receive children in need of care and protection; conduct inquiry; direct restoration; place child in care institution if needed. S.31 JJA: Powers — same as JMFC for ordering production of child. No child shall be kept in jail or police station — must be taken to CWC/Observation Home within 24 hours.","3","Child Welfare Committee","Child Protection / Juvenile",["JJA CWC S27 S31 district chairperson woman expert care protection inquiry restoration care institution JMFC 24 hours no jail police station"]],
["JJA_adoption","JJA — Adoption — CARA and eligibility","S.56-73 JJA: Adoption shall be resorted to for ensuring right of orphan, abandoned and surrendered children to family, love, care. Central Adoption Resource Authority (CARA) is nodal body. Prospective adoptive parents (PAPs) must register with CARA. Eligibility: financially stable, no life-threatening illness, age criteria (PAP age minus child's age not more than 55 years for single parent). Inter-country adoption: only when domestic adoption not possible in 2 years; Hague Convention compliance.","8","Adoption","Child Protection / Juvenile",["JJA adoption CARA S56 orphan abandoned surrendered PAPs register CARA eligibility age 55 years inter-country Hague Convention domestic first 2 years"]],
];
