/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   FILE 4 — bulk_modern_laws_judgments.json
   BNS/BNSS/BSA extended, DPDPA, key SC judgments,
   legal maxims, constitutional principles
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
const modern = [];
const MOD_DATA=[
// BNS 2023 extended
["BNS_community","BNS 2023 — Community service as punishment","Section 4 BNS 2023 introduces community service as a new category of punishment — the sixth category along with death, life imprisonment, rigorous imprisonment, simple imprisonment, fine, and forfeiture. Community service means unpaid work for the benefit of the community as may be directed by the court. Under S.23 BNS: mischief causing damage up to Rs 5,000 may be punished with community service instead of imprisonment. Under S.303(2) BNS: theft of property under Rs 5,000 — community service for first offence. Community service bridges gap between prison and fine.","2","Punishments","Criminal Law / BNS",["BNS community service 2023 sixth category unpaid work court S23 S303 theft mischief Rs 5000 first offence bridge imprisonment fine new punishment"]],
["BNS_organized","BNS 2023 — Organised crime — S.111","S.111 BNS 2023 defines organised crime: any continuing unlawful activity including kidnapping, robbery, extortion, land grabbing, contract killing, economic offences, cyber crimes, human trafficking, drug trafficking, illicit liquor trade carried out by a person singly or jointly as member of an organised crime syndicate or on behalf of such syndicate through use of violence, threat or intimidation. Death/life imprisonment if death results; 5 years to life otherwise. Syndicate defined: group of 2+ persons engaged in continuous unlawful activity.","6","Offences","Criminal Law / BNS",["BNS organised crime S111 2023 kidnapping robbery extortion land grab contract killing cyber drug trafficking syndicate 5 years life death two persons group"]],
["BNS_hit_run","BNS 2023 — Hit and run — S.106(2)","S.106(2) BNS 2023: Whoever causes death of any person by rash and negligent driving and escapes without reporting to police/Magistrate immediately after the accident shall be punished with imprisonment up to 10 years and fine. Replaces S.304A IPC (2 years) for cases where driver flees. This was controversial — truck drivers' strike in January 2024 led to suspension of implementation pending discussions with transport unions. The provision is stricter than S.279/304A IPC combination.","6","Offences","Criminal Law / BNS",["BNS hit run S106(2) 2023 rash negligent escape report 10 years truck driver strike suspended S304A IPC comparison stricter January 2024"]],
["BNS_CSAM","BNS 2023 — Child sexual abuse material — S.94","S.94 BNS 2023: Any person who distributes, publishes, exhibits, imports, exports, transmits, or stores through digital or electronic medium any obscene material involving children or engages a child for publishing such material shall be punished with rigorous imprisonment not less than 5 years but which may extend to 7 years and fine up to Rs 10 lakh; second offence: 7-10 years and fine. This provisions supplements S.67B IT Act which is being replaced by BNSS provisions.","5","Sexual Offences","Criminal Law / BNS",["BNS CSAM S94 child sexual abuse material 5 years 7 years Rs 10 lakh second offence distribute publish digital electronic import export S67B IT Act"]],
// BNSS 2023 extended
["BNSS_trial_time","BNSS 2023 — Time limit for trial","S.346 BNSS: In all cases under this Sanhita, endeavour shall be made that investigation of an offence shall be completed within sixty days. S.346(2): For Sessions trial: three years from date of framing of charge. S.346(3): For Magistrate trial: one year. S.346(4): If trial not completed in time, accused shall be released on bail unless Magistrate records reasons for further detention. This provision codifies the right to speedy trial from Article 21 into statute.","22","Trials","Criminal Procedure / BNSS",["BNSS trial time limit S346 60 days investigation Sessions 3 years Magistrate 1 year bail release reasons detention Article 21 speedy trial codified 2023"]],
["BNSS_victim2","BNSS 2023 — Enhanced victim rights","S.193 BNSS: victim or his pleader shall be given notice of final report (charge sheet or closure report). S.360 BNSS: victim/informant may examine witness with court's permission. S.489 BNSS: victim of crime shall be provided with free medical treatment. S.396 BNSS: mandatory victim compensation in all cases. S.530 BNSS: Protection of victim identity — especially in rape, POCSO cases. The BNSS significantly enhances victim participation in criminal justice.","24","Victim Rights","Criminal Procedure / BNSS",["BNSS victim rights S193 S360 S489 S396 S530 notice charge sheet medical treatment compensation identity protection rape POCSO pleader examine witness 2023"]],
["BNSS_electronic_trial","BNSS 2023 — Electronic/digital trial provisions","S.530 BNSS: All trials, inquiries and proceedings under BNSS may be held by use of audio-video electronic means or other electronic mode. S.174(2) BNSS: Investigation of 7+ year offences must include mandatory forensic examination of crime scene. S.394 BNSS: Sessions Court judgment can be communicated to accused electronically. S.105 BNSS: Proclamation and attachment can be done through electronic means. E-court integration — courts connect with police, prisons and prosecution through ICJS. This modernises the criminal justice system.","30","Electronic Proceedings","Criminal Procedure / BNSS",["BNSS electronic trial S530 audio-video digital S174 forensic mandatory S394 electronic judgment S105 proclamation e-court ICJS modernise criminal justice 2023"]],
// BSA 2023 extended
["BSA_electronic","BSA 2023 — Electronic evidence simplified","S.63 BSA 2023: Simplified electronic evidence certification — Sec officer of the company managing the device/server must give certificate. Unlike S.65B IEA where anyone in charge of the device could certify, BSA requires specific officer. The Arjun Panditrao (2020) mandatory certificate requirement is retained. The SC's Shafhi Mohammad (2017) view that certificate is not always mandatory has been overruled by Arjun Panditrao. BSA harmonises electronic evidence rules with IT Act 2000.","6","Documentary Evidence","Evidence Law / BSA",["BSA electronic evidence S63 certificate officer server Arjun Panditrao mandatory Shafhi Mohammad overruled S65B IEA harmonise IT Act 2000 2023"]],
["BSA_secondary","BSA 2023 — Secondary evidence expanded","S.57 BSA 2023: Secondary evidence means and includes certified copies; copies made by mechanical processes; copies made from original by comparing with original; counterparts of documents as against parties not executing them; oral accounts of contents of documents given by persons who have seen them. Under BSA, electronic records stored in cloud are treated as primary evidence if authenticated. Secondary evidence can be given when: original with adverse party; original lost; original cannot be produced.","8","Documentary Evidence","Evidence Law / BSA",["BSA secondary evidence S57 certified copies mechanical cloud primary authenticated adverse party lost cannot produce electronic records 2023"]],
// DPDPA 2023 extended
["DPDPA_fiduciary","DPDPA 2023 — Obligations of Data Fiduciary","S.8 DPDPA 2023: Every data fiduciary shall take reasonable security safeguards to prevent personal data breach; notify Data Protection Board and each affected data principal of any breach; erase personal data as soon as the purpose is served (right to be forgotten); ensure accuracy and completeness of data; establish mechanism for grievances. Significant data fiduciaries (notified by Central Government) have additional obligations: appoint DPO (Data Protection Officer), conduct DPIA (Data Protection Impact Assessment), independent audit.","2","Obligations","Cyber Law / Data Protection",["DPDPA data fiduciary S8 security safeguards breach notify erase right to be forgotten accuracy grievance significant DPO DPIA audit 2023"]],
["DPDPA_board","DPDPA 2023 — Data Protection Board of India","S.18 DPDPA 2023: Data Protection Board of India established. Functions: receive and decide complaints from data principals; take suo motu action on breach; impose penalties; order compensation. S.25: Penalties — up to Rs 250 crore for security safeguards; Rs 200 crore for notification failure; Rs 200 crore for children data obligation breach; Rs 10,000 crore for breach of voluntary undertaking. The Board is a digital office — all proceedings conducted online. Appeals to TDSAT (Telecom Disputes Settlement and Appellate Tribunal).","6","Enforcement","Cyber Law / Data Protection",["DPDPA Data Protection Board S18 S25 complaint suo motu penalty Rs 250 crore Rs 200 crore Rs 10000 crore children compensation digital TDSAT appeal 2023"]],
// Landmark SC Judgments (detailed)
["SCJ_Olga","Olga Tellis v. Bombay Municipal Corporation (1985)","Right to livelihood is part of Article 21. Pavement dwellers in Mumbai challenged BMC's action of demolishing their huts. SC held: right to life includes right to livelihood; eviction without alternative arrangement violates Article 21. But: right to livelihood is not absolute — can be restricted by law. The 7-day notice given was held insufficient. The Court directed that proper procedure must be followed before evictions. This case expanded Article 21 to socio-economic rights.","Special","Landmark Judgments","Constitutional Law",["Olga Tellis livelihood Article 21 1985 pavement dwellers BMC eviction procedure notice socio-economic right life expanded"]],
["SCJ_Nilabati","Nilabati Behera v. State of Orissa (1993)","Custodial death — compensation under public law. SC held that under Article 32/226 (constitutional torts), the State is liable to pay compensation for violation of fundamental rights. Compensation under public law is different from private law (tort) damages. The rule of sovereign immunity does not apply to compensation under constitutional law. This case established the principle that courts can grant monetary compensation for constitutional violations without relegating victim to civil suit.","Special","Landmark Judgments","Constitutional Law",["Nilabati Behera custodial death 1993 compensation Article 32 Article 226 public law constitutional tort sovereign immunity no civil suit monetary"]],
["SCJ_Hussainara","Hussainara Khatoon v. State of Bihar (1979)","Right to speedy trial is fundamental right under Article 21. SC noticed that thousands of under-trial prisoners had been in jail for periods longer than maximum punishment for the offence. Directed release of such under-trials. Right to legal aid for poor accused is also a fundamental right. Courts have duty to inform accused of legal aid. Hussainara Khatoon is foundation of all prisoner rights jurisprudence in India.","Special","Landmark Judgments","Constitutional Law",["Hussainara Khatoon 1979 speedy trial Article 21 under-trial prisoners maximum sentence legal aid fundamental right prisoner rights Bihar"]],
["SCJ_Francis","Francis Coralie Mullin v. Union Territory of Delhi (1981)","Right to live with human dignity includes right to basic necessities of life — food, clothing, shelter; right to read and write; right of movement; right to basic health. SC: Article 21 does not mean mere animal existence. The expression 'life' in Article 21 must be read expansively to include all facets of human dignity. Solitary confinement and denial of basic amenities violate Article 21.","Special","Landmark Judgments","Constitutional Law",["Francis Coralie 1981 dignity Article 21 food clothing shelter read write movement health animal existence expansive human dignity solitary confinement"]],
["SCJ_Vishaka2","Vishaka v. State of Rajasthan (1997) — detailed","SC laid down Vishaka guidelines: (1) Employer's duty to prohibit sexual harassment and frame policy; (2) Committee must include at least 50% women, presided by woman; (3) Committee must include NGO representative; (4) Complaints to be time-bound; (5) Awareness to be created. The guidelines were interim measures till Parliament enacted law. POSH Act 2013 replaced Vishaka guidelines. The case was filed by Naina Kapur after Bhanwari Devi gang rape (Rajasthan, 1992). Extended sexual harassment definition beyond physical to verbal, non-verbal.","Special","Landmark Judgments","Constitutional Law",["Vishaka 1997 guidelines sexual harassment employer committee NGO POSH Act 2013 replaced Bhanwari Devi Article 14 15 21 verbal non-verbal awareness"]],
["SCJ_NALSA2","NALSA v. Union of India (2014) — detailed","SC recognised transgender persons as the 'third gender'. Self-identification: the State cannot insist on any particular procedure for gender recognition — it is a matter of self-determination. SC applied right to identity, dignity, expression under Articles 14, 15, 16, 19, 21. Directed: (1) recognise third gender; (2) extend reservation benefits; (3) provide medical care; (4) prohibit discrimination. Transgender Persons (Protection of Rights) Act 2019 enacted subsequently. SC in Supriyo v. UOI (2023) refused to constitutionalise same-sex marriage — left to Parliament.","Special","Landmark Judgments","Constitutional Law",["NALSA transgender 2014 third gender self-identification dignity Articles 14 15 16 19 21 reservation medical care Transgender Act 2019 Supriyo same-sex marriage Parliament"]],
["SCJ_Puttaswamy2","KS Puttaswamy v. Union of India (2017) — Privacy judgment detailed","9-judge bench unanimously held right to privacy is fundamental under Article 21. The right includes: autonomy (decisional, personal, bodily); informational privacy; dignity. The court overruled MP Sharma (1954) and Kharak Singh (1963) which had held no fundamental right to privacy. Privacy can be limited by: law; legitimate aim; proportionality; procedural guarantees. The judgment became foundation for: DPDPA 2023; Aadhaar challenge; LGBTQ rights (Section 377); data localisation debates. Justice DY Chandrachud (as he then was) authored the key concurrence.","Special","Landmark Judgments","Constitutional Law",["Puttaswamy privacy 2017 fundamental right Article 21 9-judge bench autonomy decisional bodily informational dignity MP Sharma Kharak Singh overruled proportionality DPDPA Aadhaar LGBTQ Chandrachud"]],
["SCJ_Shreya","Shreya Singhal v. Union of India (2015)","SC struck down S.66A IT Act as unconstitutional for violating Article 19(1)(a) (freedom of speech and expression). S.66A was vague — terms like 'grossly offensive', 'menacing character', 'annoyance' were undefined. SC held: (a) a law restricting free speech must be within Art.19(2) grounds; (b) S.66A did not fall within any Art.19(2) ground; (c) the provision was over-broad. S.69A (blocking of websites) upheld as having procedural safeguards. This case is landmark for internet freedom in India. SC in 2023 asked States to stop applying S.66A.","Special","Landmark Judgments","Constitutional Law",["Shreya Singhal 2015 Section 66A IT Act struck down Article 19(1)(a) vague grossly offensive menacing annoyance S69A website blocking internet freedom 2023"]],
["SCJ_Joseph_shine","Joseph Shine v. Union of India (2018)","SC unanimously struck down S.497 IPC (adultery) as unconstitutional. Held: (a) it treated women as property of husbands; (b) violated Articles 14 (equality), 15 (non-discrimination on basis of sex), 21 (dignity and autonomy); (c) wife's consent was irrelevant — treated husband as aggrieved party; (d) archaic provision based on 19th century morality. Adultery removed as criminal offence — may still be ground for divorce. Chief Justice Dipak Misra, Justice DY Chandrachud wrote separate concurrences.","Special","Landmark Judgments","Constitutional Law",["Joseph Shine 2018 adultery S497 IPC struck down unconstitutional Articles 14 15 21 property husband consent archaic divorce ground Dipak Misra Chandrachud"]],
["SCJ_Abhilasha","Abhilasha v. Prakash (2020) — Maintenance for unmarried daughter","SC held: Hindu Succession Act S.6 as amended in 2005 gives equal rights to daughters as coparceners. A Hindu daughter can seek maintenance from father under S.125 CrPC even after attaining majority if she is unable to maintain herself. Sections 20 and 22 HAMA: parents must maintain children. S.20 HAMA: children legitimate/illegitimate can claim maintenance as long as minor. Adult daughters: maintenance only if unable to maintain themselves due to physical/mental illness or for education.","Special","Landmark Judgments","Family Law",["Abhilasha 2020 maintenance daughter S125 CrPC HAMA S20 S22 HSA coparcener unmarried adult physical mental illness education father"]],
["SCJ_Rajnesh","Rajnesh v. Neha and Another (2020) — Maintenance guidelines","SC issued comprehensive guidelines for maintenance across all personal laws: (1) mandatory disclosure of assets; (2) uniform criteria for maintenance; (3) retroactivity — maintenance from date of application; (4) no double recovery for same period; (5) enforcement mechanism — imprisonment in default; (6) all maintenance proceedings to be disposed within 6 months. These guidelines apply to S.125 CrPC, HMA S.24 & S.25, HAMA, DV Act. Aim: uniformity and effective enforcement of maintenance.","Special","Landmark Judgments","Family Law",["Rajnesh Neha 2020 maintenance guidelines SC disclosure assets uniform criteria retroactive from application date double recovery 6 months CrPC HMA HAMA DV Act enforcement"]],
// Legal Maxims (50 key maxims)
["MAXIM_1","Actus non facit reum nisi mens sit rea","An act does not make a person guilty unless the mind be guilty. This is the fundamental principle of criminal law — both actus reus (guilty act) and mens rea (guilty mind) are required for criminal liability. Strict liability offences (like under NDPS for possession) are exceptions. IPC sections distinguish between intention, knowledge, and recklessness. This maxim is the foundation of the fault-based system of criminal justice in India.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Criminal Law",["actus reus mens rea guilty act mind criminal liability strict liability intention knowledge recklessness IPC fault-based maxim"]],
["MAXIM_2","Audi alteram partem","Hear the other side — no one shall be condemned unheard. This is a principle of natural justice. S.34 IPC: opportunity to represent before action taken. All quasi-judicial bodies must follow this principle. Violation: decision is void. The Supreme Court in Maneka Gandhi extended this to include a meaningful hearing — a hearing that is fair and reasonable.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Administrative Law",["audi alteram partem hear other side natural justice condemned unheard quasi-judicial void fair meaningful Maneka Gandhi administrative law"]],
["MAXIM_3","Nemo debet esse judex in propria causa","No one should be a judge in his own cause — rule against bias. Any judge, arbitrator, or authority that has a personal interest in the outcome must disqualify himself. Types of bias: pecuniary bias, personal bias, official bias, subject-matter bias. The test: real danger of bias or reasonable suspicion. An administrative decision tainted by bias is void. High Court/SC can quash biased decisions under Article 226/32.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Administrative Law",["nemo judex propria causa judge own cause bias natural justice pecuniary personal official subject-matter real danger disqualify quash Article 226 void"]],
["MAXIM_4","Ex turpi causa non oritur actio","No action arises from a base cause. A claimant cannot recover if his claim arises from an illegal act. This applies in contract law: an agreement which is against law or public policy is void. Example: a party cannot sue for proceeds of an illegal contract. In tort law: a trespasser who gets injured while committing crime cannot claim full damages. Limited by: proportionality — the turpitude must be causally connected to the harm.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Civil Law",["ex turpi causa illegal contract void public policy trespasser damages proportionality causally connected recovery barred maxim civil law"]],
["MAXIM_5","Ignorantia juris non excusat","Ignorance of law is no excuse. Every person is presumed to know the law. A defendant cannot plead ignorance of the law as a defence. Exceptions: S.76 IPC — mistake of fact (not law) is a defence. Foreign law: a person acting on foreign law in India may argue ignorance if they acted in good faith. This principle ensures that laws are uniformly applicable and prevents fraudulent claims of ignorance.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Criminal Law",["ignorantia juris law excuse presumed know exception mistake fact S76 IPC foreign law good faith uniform fraudulent ignorance maxim"]],
["MAXIM_6","Res ipsa loquitur","The thing speaks for itself. In negligence cases, where the circumstances of the accident point strongly to the defendant's negligence and the exact cause cannot be proved, the mere happening of the accident creates a presumption of negligence. The burden shifts to the defendant to explain. Applied in: medical negligence (Bolan test + res ipsa), road accidents, defective products. Not a rule of law but a rule of evidence — an evidentiary shortcut.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Tort / Civil Law",["res ipsa loquitur negligence presumption accident burden shift defendant medical Bolan test road defective product evidentiary shortcut maxim tort"]],
["MAXIM_7","In pari delicto potior est conditio possidentis","When both parties are equally at fault, the one in possession is in a better position. Applied in illegal contracts: where both parties are equally guilty of illegal act, neither can recover. The law leaves them where it finds them. Contrast: where parties are not in pari delicto, the less guilty party may recover. Related: ex turpi causa — one in possession retains advantage.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Civil Law",["in pari delicto equally fault possession better position illegal contract both guilty neither recover leaves them maxim civil law contract"]],
["MAXIM_8","Falsus in uno, falsus in omnibus","False in one thing, false in everything. If a witness is found to be lying about one material fact, the court may discard the entire testimony. However, Indian courts have moderated this: courts may accept part of testimony and reject part. SC has held this is not an absolute rule in India — court can act on partly credible testimony.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Evidence Law",["falsus in uno witness false one material fact discard testimony Indian courts moderated partly credible not absolute rule evidence maxim"]],
["MAXIM_9","Ubi jus ibi remedium","Where there is a right, there is a remedy. If a legal right is violated, the law must provide a remedy. This is the foundation of judicial review under Articles 32 and 226. Applied in: enforcement of fundamental rights; administrative law review; constitutional remedies. The maxim ensures that no right exists in vacuum — every right must have an enforceable remedy.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Constitutional Law",["ubi jus ibi remedium right remedy violation judicial review Article 32 Article 226 fundamental rights administrative constitutional enforceable vacuum"]],
["MAXIM_10","Salus populi suprema lex","The welfare of the people is the supreme law. The State can restrict individual rights for the greater good of society. Foundation for: reasonable restrictions under Article 19(2)-(6); public interest legislation; police powers; eminent domain. Balanced against individual rights — restrictions must be proportionate. This maxim is frequently cited in cases involving public health emergencies, environmental law, land acquisition.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Constitutional Law",["salus populi welfare people supreme law restrict individual rights greater good Article 19(2) public interest police powers eminent domain proportionate emergency environment acquisition"]],
["MAXIM_11","Qui facit per alium facit per se","He who does through another does through himself. Principle of vicarious liability — employer is responsible for acts of employee done in course of employment. Applied in: master-servant liability in tort; principal-agent relationship in contract; Section 149 IPC (vicarious liability of unlawful assembly member). Also foundation for corporate liability — acts of directors/employees bind the company.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Tort / Criminal Law",["qui facit per alium vicarious liability employer employee course of employment tort master servant principal agent S149 IPC corporate directors bind maxim"]],
["MAXIM_12","Suppressio veri, suggestio falsi","Suppression of truth is equivalent to suggestion of falsehood. Non-disclosure of material fact is as bad as making a false statement — both can vitiate consent and make a contract voidable. Applied in: insurance (utmost good faith — uberrimae fidei); contract (misrepresentation by omission); equity (clean hands doctrine). A party seeking equitable relief must come with clean hands.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Contract / Equity Law",["suppressio veri suggestio falsi suppression truth equivalent falsehood non-disclosure material fact vitiates consent contract voidable insurance uberrimae fidei equity clean hands"]],
["MAXIM_13","Delegatus non potest delegare","A delegate cannot further delegate. An agent cannot sub-delegate authority entrusted to him unless: (1) custom of trade permits; (2) nature of agency requires it; (3) principal expressly or impliedly permits. Applied in: S.190 ICA — agent cannot lawfully employ another unless permitted. Also in administrative law: a body to which powers are delegated cannot further sub-delegate without specific authority in the parent statute.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Contract / Administrative Law",["delegatus non potest delegare delegate further authority agent sub-delegate custom permit S190 ICA administrative sub-delegation parent statute authority"]],
["MAXIM_14","Pacta sunt servanda","Agreements must be kept. The principle that parties to a valid contract are bound to honour its terms. This is the fundamental principle of contract law codified in S.37 ICA. Exceptions: S.56 ICA (frustration/impossibility); force majeure clauses; breach by other party; illegal contracts. Applied in international law as a foundational principle of treaties under Vienna Convention on Law of Treaties 1969.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Contract Law",["pacta sunt servanda agreements must be kept contract bound honour terms S37 ICA frustration S56 force majeure Vienna Convention international treaties fundamental"]],
["MAXIM_15","Volenti non fit injuria","To one who is willing, no harm is done. A person who voluntarily takes a risk cannot sue for damages arising from that risk. Applied in: sports participants; spectators at events; medical patients who consent to treatment; persons assuming obvious risks. Limitations: the consent must be free and informed (not under economic pressure); consent to risk ≠ consent to negligence; consent of child may be invalid.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Tort Law",["volenti non fit injuria voluntary risk cannot sue sports spectators consent treatment obvious limitation free informed economic pressure child invalid maxim tort"]],
["MAXIM_16","De minimis non curat lex","The law does not concern itself with trifles. Courts will not entertain trivial claims or impose liability for de minimis violations. Applied in: court not entertaining frivolous PILs; minor technical violations of procedural rules; small defects in charge (S.215 CrPC); slight variations in auction bids. SC has used this maxim to dismiss frivolous contempt petitions and minor irregularities in elections.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Civil / Criminal Law",["de minimis non curat lex trifles trivial courts not entertain frivolous PIL procedural minor defect charge S215 CrPC auction contempt election irregularity"]],
["MAXIM_17","Noscitur a sociis","A word is known by its associates. In statutory interpretation: the meaning of an ambiguous word in a statute should be understood in the context of the surrounding words. A list of words of the same class — the last ambiguous word takes its colour from the rest. Applied in: S.19 IEA (facts necessary to explain/introduce) — 'explain' read with surrounding context; constitutional interpretation of 'other right' in Article 19.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Interpretation",["noscitur a sociis statutory interpretation ambiguous word context surrounding class list colour Article 19 S19 IEA ejusdem generis related"]],
["MAXIM_18","Ejusdem generis","Of the same kind. Where a general word follows specific words of the same class, the general word is limited to things of the same class. Example: 'vehicles used on public roads: cars, buses, trucks and other vehicles' — 'other vehicles' limited to road vehicles, not ships or aircraft. Applied extensively in Indian statutory interpretation. Must have a genus — if items are of different classes, ejusdem generis does not apply.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Interpretation",["ejusdem generis same kind general word specific class limited interpretation statute example vehicles public road genus different classes India"]],
["MAXIM_19","Expressio unius est exclusio alterius","The expression of one thing is the exclusion of the other. In statutory interpretation: when a statute expressly mentions certain items, it implicitly excludes all others. Example: if a section says 'A, B, and C are exempt', D is not exempt. Counter-principle: sometimes the list is illustrative, not exhaustive — use of 'include' suggests wider meaning. Courts balance expressio unius against legislative intent.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Interpretation",["expressio unius exclusio alterius expression one exclusion other statutory interpretation explicit list exempt illustrative exhaustive include legislative intent courts balance"]],
["MAXIM_20","Ut res magis valeat quam pereat","It is better for a thing to have effect than to be made void. In statutory interpretation: prefer the interpretation that gives effect to a provision over one that makes it redundant or void. Courts avoid making a statutory provision meaningless. Applied in: constitutional provisions — court reads a provision to be valid rather than unconstitutional if possible; contractual interpretation — save the contract rather than void it.","Legal Maxims","Latin Maxims","Interpretation",["ut res magis valeat effect void interpretation give effect redundant constitutional valid save contract statutory provision meaningless courts avoid"]],
];
