/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   FILE 5 — bulk_environment_criminal.json
   NDPS, Environment, Wildlife, Arms, UAPA, PMLA, JJA,
   Prevention of Corruption, Public Safety
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
const envCrim = [];
const ENV_DATA=[
// NDPS Act detailed sections
["NDPS_5","Prohibition of cultivating cannabis plant or coca plant","S.8(a) NDPS: No person shall cultivate any coca plant or gather any portion of a coca plant. S.8(b): cultivate or use cannabis except for medical/scientific purposes. Cannabis means the separated resin (charas), the flowering or fruiting tops of cannabis plant (ganja), and any mixture or drink prepared from them (bhang). Bhang regulation is left to States under State Excise laws. Cultivation of cannabis even for personal use is prohibited.","4","Prohibition","Criminal Law / Drug Offences",["NDPS cultivation cannabis coca S8 charas ganja bhang bhang State Excise personal use prohibited section 5 section 8"]],
["NDPS_28","Preparation for commission of offence","S.28 NDPS: Whoever makes preparation to commit an offence under the provisions of this Act shall be punishable with half the punishment provided for the offence. Preparation is different from attempt — preparation is one step before attempt. For NDPS offences, even preparation is severely punished. This provision ensures that persons caught in the planning stage before the actual offence can be prosecuted.","5","Offences","Criminal Law / Drug Offences",["NDPS preparation offence section 28 half punishment attempt different planning stage prosecuted"]],
["NDPS_29","Abetment and criminal conspiracy","S.29 NDPS: Whoever abets, or is a party to a criminal conspiracy to commit, an offence punishable under this Act shall, whether such offence be or be not committed in consequence of such abetment or in pursuance of such criminal conspiracy, and notwithstanding anything contained in section 116 of the Indian Penal Code, be punishable with the punishment provided for the offence.","5","Offences","Criminal Law / Drug Offences",["NDPS abetment criminal conspiracy section 29 offence punishable IPC section 116 committed not committed punishment"]],
["NDPS_32A","No suspension remission commutation of sentence","S.32A NDPS: Notwithstanding anything contained in the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973, or any other law for the time being in force but subject to the provisions of S.33, no sentence awarded under this Act (other than S.26 and S.27) shall be suspended or remitted or commuted. This means an NDPS convict serving sentence cannot get premature release — the sentence must be fully served.","5","Offences","Criminal Law / Drug Offences",["NDPS no remission suspension commutation section 32A full sentence serve CrPC exception S26 S27 premature release barred"]],
["NDPS_34","Immunity of witness","S.34 NDPS: An approver (accomplice who turns State witness) is immune from prosecution if he makes full disclosure. The court may tender pardon to an accomplice. This is similar to S.306 CrPC provision for pardon. Approver evidence: must be corroborated — SC has held in NDPS cases that sole approver evidence without corroboration is insufficient for conviction.","5","Offences","Criminal Law / Drug Offences",["NDPS approver immunity section 34 pardon full disclosure witness S306 CrPC corroboration sole approver insufficient conviction"]],
["NDPS_36C","Summary trial of certain offences","S.36C NDPS: Notwithstanding anything contained in the CrPC, all offences under this Act which are punishable with imprisonment for a term of not more than three years may be tried summarily. NDPS summary trial can be completed quickly. But most NDPS cases involving commercial quantity are tried before Special Courts under S.36A.","5","Offences","Criminal Law / Drug Offences",["NDPS summary trial section 36C three years CrPC Special Court commercial quantity S36A"]],
["NDPS_39","Immunity of informant","S.39 NDPS: Information furnished by an informant to any officer under this Act, if in the interests of justice the court so decides, need not be disclosed to the accused. This protects informants from retribution. The court has wide discretion. However, if non-disclosure prejudices the accused's right to fair trial, disclosure may be ordered.","5","Offences","Criminal Law / Drug Offences",["NDPS informant immunity section 39 protection disclosure accused interests justice court discretion fair trial"]],
["NDPS_46","Punishment for vexatious entry, search, seizure, arrest","S.46 NDPS: Any officer exercising powers under this Act who without reasonable ground enters or searches or arrests any person shall be punished with imprisonment up to six months or fine up to one thousand rupees or both. This provision is to prevent abuse of power by enforcement agencies.","7","Miscellaneous","Criminal Law / Drug Offences",["NDPS vexatious entry search seizure arrest section 46 without reasonable ground six months one thousand officer abuse power"]],
["NDPS_55","Procedure for seized articles","S.55 NDPS: Officer who seizes any narcotic drug, psychotropic substance, controlled substance or conveyance or article shall immediately give information to his superior officer. The property shall be disposed of as prescribed. Proper sampling and preservation is critical — defective sampling can lead to acquittal. SC has held that violation of S.55 does not automatically lead to acquittal — effect depends on prejudice caused.","8","Procedure","Criminal Law / Drug Offences",["NDPS seized articles section 55 inform superior sampling preservation defective acquittal prejudice disposal conveyance procedure"]],
// Wildlife Protection Act extended
["WPA_schedule","Wildlife Protection Act — Schedules","Schedule I animals (highest protection — punishment up to 7 years): tiger, lion, leopard, elephant, rhinoceros, bear, crocodile, python, sea horse, whale, dolphins. Schedule II: animals like wolf, otter, monitor lizard, porcupine. Schedule III/IV: less protected. Schedule V: vermin (can be hunted). Schedule VI: plants (orchids, pitcher plants). Committees exist to move animals between schedules. After 2022 amendment: only 4 schedules; animals requiring special protection in Schedule I, migratory animals in Schedule II.","3","Schedules","Environmental Law / Wildlife",["WPA schedule I tiger lion elephant rhinoceros python Schedule II IV V VI vermin plants orchid 2022 amendment four schedules migrate special protection"]],
["WPA_sanctuary","WPA — Declaring and managing a wildlife sanctuary — S.18-26","S.18 WPA: State Government may by notification declare any area to be a Wildlife Sanctuary. S.19-26: powers of Chief Wildlife Warden; rights settlement; controlled and restricted activities within; forestry operations only with Chief Warden's permission. S.24: collector shall determine rights of persons within sanctuary. Key: once declared, no hunting, felling of trees, quarrying, grazing above prescribed limits. Private land can be included in sanctuary after rights settlement.","4","Sanctuaries","Environmental Law / Wildlife",["WPA sanctuary S18 S19 S26 State Government notification Chief Wildlife Warden rights settlement private land hunting felling quarrying grazing restricted"]],
["WPA_trade2","WPA — Regulation of trade in wild animals","S.44-48 WPA: No person shall business or trade in any wild animal, animal article, trophy, or trophy of such animal without a licence. Declaration of stocks; record-keeping; transport permits; sale to licensed persons only. S.49B: Prohibition of trade in specified plants. Ivory trade: S.43 WPA — no ivory trade allowed except for pre-commencement of Act ivory. CITES (Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species) compliance mandatory for exports/imports.","5","Trade","Environmental Law / Wildlife",["WPA trade regulation S44 S48 S49B licence trophy animal article ivory CITES export import stock declaration transport permit plants specified"]],
["WPA_prosecution","WPA — Prosecution and burden of proof","S.55 WPA: Where any person is found or alleged to have committed an offence under this Act, and it is proved that such person was found or was in possession of Schedule I animal, article or trophy, the court shall presume that such person has committed the offence unless he proves the contrary. Reverse burden of proof — accused must show he had the animal lawfully. Bail in WPA Schedule I offences is difficult.","7","Prosecution","Environmental Law / Wildlife",["WPA reverse burden proof section 55 Schedule I possession presumption offence committed prove contrary bail difficult accused"]],
// Environmental Laws extended
["EPA_EIA","Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA) — EPA 1986","Under EPA 1986, Government notified EIA Notification 2006: all new projects in specified categories must obtain environmental clearance before commencing. The process: screening, scoping, public hearing, appraisal. Expert Appraisal Committee (EAC) recommends; MoEF grants EC (Environmental Clearance). EC conditions must be complied with. Coastal Regulation Zone (CRZ) Notification 2019 — controls activities near coast. Environmental violations can attract criminal prosecution under EPA.","3","Environmental Clearance","Environmental Law",["EIA environmental clearance EPA 1986 EIA Notification 2006 screening scoping public hearing EAC MoEF EC CRZ 2019 coastal criminal prosecution violation"]],
["EPA_polluter","Polluter pays principle — extended application","National Green Tribunal has extensively applied polluter pays principle: (1) Apollo Tyres case: factory discharging untreated effluent — directed pay Rs 1 crore compensation; (2) BBMB case: dam displacing tribals; (3) Ganga pollution — multiple companies directed to pay. The principle now extended to: historical pollution (clean up past contamination); ecological damage (beyond just compensation); public health costs. NGT can order closure + compensation simultaneously.","3","Principles","Environmental Law",["NGT polluter pays Apollo Tyres BBMB Ganga closure compensation historical pollution ecological damage public health NGT order simultaneously extended application"]],
["WPA_TIGER","Project Tiger / Project Elephant — Wildlife Conservation","Project Tiger launched 1973 — core scheme for tiger conservation in India. Currently 53 tiger reserves. National Tiger Conservation Authority (NTCA) — S.38O WPA. Core zone: inviolate, no human activity. Buffer zone: co-existence area. SC in TN Godavarman: buffer zone activities regulated. India has 70% of world's wild tigers (2023 census: 3167 tigers). Project Elephant 1992: 33 elephant reserves. Wildlife Crime Control Bureau (WCCB) — to control wildlife trafficking.","Special","Projects","Environmental Law / Wildlife",["Project Tiger 1973 53 reserves NTCA S38O WPA core buffer zone SC Godavarman India 70 percent 3167 tigers 2023 Project Elephant 33 reserves WCCB wildlife trafficking"]],
// Arms Act extended
["ARMS_NPB","Arms Act — Non-prohibited bore vs prohibited bore","Non-prohibited bore (NPB) firearms: ordinary country-made pistols, revolvers, rifles with bore not prohibited. Citizens can hold NPB arms with licence. Prohibited bore (PB) arms: include .303 Lee Enfield, AK-47, SLR, weapons used by military/paramilitary. S.7 Arms Act: No person shall acquire, have in possession or carry any prohibited arms or ammunition except under Central Government licence. Illicit trade in prohibited arms attracts S.25(1AA) — minimum 7 years, max life imprisonment.","2","Classification","Criminal Law / Arms",["Arms Act prohibited bore non-prohibited NPB PB AK-47 SLR .303 Central Government S7 S25 seven years life imprisonment licence classification ordinary pistol"]],
["ARMS_surrender","Arms Act — Surrender and deposit of arms","S.21 Arms Act: The licensing authority may, by written order, require any person holding a licence to deposit the arms, ammunition or explosive substance in the nearest police station pending the disposal of any application under the Act. Where a person's licence is revoked or not renewed, he shall within the prescribed period deposit the arms at nearest police station. Illegal retention after revocation of licence attracts S.25 penalties.","3","Licensing","Criminal Law / Arms",["Arms Act surrender deposit S21 licensing authority police station revocation not renewed prescribed period illegal retention S25 penalty written order"]],
// UAPA extended
["UAPA_frozen","UAPA — Freezing of funds and funds relating to terrorism","S.25 UAPA: Any property which may be connected with any terrorist act or with a terrorist organisation may be seized, frozen, or confiscated. S.25(1A): The Central Government may, if it has reason to believe that funds of a person are likely to be used for a terrorist act, by order in writing, freeze such funds. Freezing orders: must be confirmed by UAPA Tribunal within 30 days. The freezing mechanism is linked to UNSC Resolution 1267 (Al Qaeda sanctions).","4","Assets","Criminal Law / Terrorism",["UAPA frozen funds S25 terrorist act organisation confiscate freeze Central Government written order Tribunal 30 days UNSC 1267 Al Qaeda sanctions"]],
["UAPA_designated","UAPA — Designated terrorist — S.35 amendment 2019","2019 Amendment to UAPA: for the first time, UAPA enables designating an individual as a terrorist (S.35). Earlier only organisations could be designated. Process: Central Government adds individual to Schedule IV by notification. No prior hearing required. Individual can make application for de-listing to the Review Committee. SC in Jaish Mohammed petition: upheld the provision but directed procedural safeguards. Designated individuals include arrested accused.","6","Terrorist Organisations","Criminal Law / Terrorism",["UAPA individual terrorist S35 2019 amendment Schedule IV notification no prior hearing de-listing Review Committee SC procedural safeguards Jaish Mohammed"]],
// Prevention of Corruption Act extended
["PCA_sanction2","PCA — Prior sanction for prosecution of serving officers","S.19 PCA 1988: Prior sanction of competent authority is mandatory before taking cognizance of offences under S.7, S.11, S.13, S.15 by public servant. S.19(1): Central Government for Central servants; State Government for State servants. S.19(3): court must record reasons for grant/refusal. Subramanian Swamy v. Manmohan Singh (2012): SC held that prior sanction is a mandatory procedural safeguard — absence vitiates prosecution. Single directive (requiring Central Government permission) struck down by SC.","4","Sanction","Anti-Corruption",["PCA prior sanction S19 Central Government State public servant cognizance Subramanian Swamy Manmohan Singh 2012 mandatory procedural safeguard single directive struck down"]],
["PCA_trap","PCA — Trap cases — Panchnama requirements","In trap cases under PCA: (a) shadow witnesses (panchas) must be present throughout; (b) phenolphthalein test or sodium carbonate solution used to colour money; (c) Demand and acceptance of bribe must be proved; (d) Bribe recovered from accused person's hands/possession. Conviction based solely on complainant and police witnesses without independent panchas is weak. Mahesh Kumar Balachandran (2018 SC): shadow witnesses' evidence crucial; independent witness preferred over police.","3","Investigation","Anti-Corruption",["PCA trap case panchnama panchas shadow witnesses phenolphthalein sodium carbonate demand acceptance recovery independent police Mahesh Kumar 2018 SC"]],
["PCA_disproportionate","PCA — Disproportionate assets — S.13(1)(e)","S.13(1)(e) PCA: A public servant commits criminal misconduct if he is in possession of pecuniary resources or property disproportionate to his known sources of income which cannot be satisfactorily explained. The prosecution must: (1) prove possession of resources beyond known income; (2) establish quantum disproportionate to income; (3) accused must then explain to court's satisfaction. If explanation unsatisfactory, conviction follows. Disproportionate: more than 10-15% above known income generally triggers charges.","3","Offences","Anti-Corruption",["PCA disproportionate assets S13(1)(e) pecuniary resources property known income explained prosecution quantum conviction 10-15 percent criminal misconduct"]],
// Juvenile Justice extended
["JJA_heinous","JJA — Heinous offences by 16-18 year olds — procedure","S.15 JJA: Board conducts preliminary assessment of 16-18 year old child for heinous offences (minimum 7 years imprisonment). Assessment: mental and physical capacity; ability to understand consequences; circumstances. Board may transfer to Children's Court (S.18(3)) for trial as adult. S.19: Children's Court may decide to try as adult — if so, child placed in place of safety (not adult jail) during trial. After conviction, sentence in reformatory till 21, then transferred to jail if sentence remains. Children's Act 2019: minimum 7 years = heinous.","2","Board","Child Protection / Juvenile",["JJA heinous S15 S18 S19 16-18 preliminary assessment Children's Court try as adult place of safety reformatory 21 years jail transfer minimum 7 years 2019"]],
["JJA_CNCP","JJA — Child in Need of Care and Protection (CNCP)","S.2(14) JJA: CNCP means a child who: is found without home; is found working in violation of labour laws; is found begging; has been exploited; is a mentally ill child; is a child at risk of marriage; is a child victim of armed conflict; is living with a person who has killed or abused another child. S.37: CWC to dispose of CNCP cases within 4 months. S.44: Restoration to family if in child's best interest. S.46-51: Sponsorship and foster care for CNCP.","3","CNCP","Child Protection / Juvenile",["JJA CNCP S2(14) S37 S44 S46 child without home begging exploited mental illness forced marriage armed conflict CWC four months restore family sponsorship foster care"]],
// PMLA extended
["PMLA_offence2","PMLA — Definition of proceeds of crime — expanded","S.2(u) PMLA: 'Proceeds of crime' means property derived or obtained, directly or indirectly, by any person as a result of criminal activity relating to a scheduled offence, or the value of any such property. After 2012 and 2015 amendments: proceeds of crime include proceeds held in India or abroad, converted assets, property to which the accused is not entitled. The SC in Vijay Madanlal Choudhary (2022) upheld that even if the predicate offence (scheduled offence) is not proved, PMLA offence can be sustained independently.","2","Offences","Criminal Law / Financial",["PMLA proceeds crime S2(u) criminal activity scheduled offence India abroad converted accused 2012 2015 amendments Vijay Madanlal 2022 predicate offence independent"]],
["PMLA_ED","PMLA — Enforcement Directorate — Powers","Enforcement Directorate (ED) under PMLA has powers to: search and seize property connected with money laundering; arrest and remand in custody; conduct raids on properties; attach properties; summon any person for examination; issue Look Out Circular (LOC) for persons likely to flee. ED can arrest without FIR — on ECIR (Enforcement Case Information Report). SC in V. Senthil Balaji (2023): ED arrest under PMLA is valid even without registering FIR for scheduled offence.","4","Enforcement","Criminal Law / Financial",["PMLA ED Enforcement Directorate search seizure arrest attach summon LOC ECIR no FIR Senthil Balaji 2023 SC valid arrest ECIR"]],
["PMLA_bail2","PMLA — Twin conditions for bail — S.45","S.45 PMLA (original): No person accused of PMLA offence shall be released on bail unless PP given opportunity and court satisfied (a) reasonable grounds to believe accused not guilty; (b) not likely to commit offence while on bail. This 'twin test' is extremely difficult to satisfy — creates near-absolute bar. SC in Nikesh Tarachand Shah (2017) struck down S.45 as unconstitutional. Parliament re-enacted S.45 without the earlier distinction. SC upheld re-enacted S.45 in Vijay Madanlal (2022).","4","Bail","Criminal Law / Financial",["PMLA bail twin conditions S45 PP reasonable grounds not guilty not commit Nikesh Tarachand 2017 struck down Parliament re-enacted Vijay Madanlal 2022 upheld difficult"]],
// Benami Transactions Act
["BTPA2","Benami Transactions Act — Appellate Mechanism","S.42-52 BTPA: The adjudicating authority under BTPA passes order of confiscation. Appeal lies to Appellate Tribunal. Further appeal to High Court on questions of law. S.53 BTPA: Punishment — not less than 1 year to 7 years + 25% of fair market value as fine. The 2016 Amendment strengthened BTPA — reduced definition of exceptions. SC in Union of India v. Ganpati Dealcom (2022) upheld 2016 amendments as prospective only.","7","Appeals","Criminal Law / Property",["BTPA adjudicating authority confiscation appellate Tribunal High Court S53 1 year 7 years 25 percent fair market value 2016 amendment Ganpati Dealcom 2022 prospective"]],
// Anti-terrorism
["IPC_114A_detail","Scheduled Offences under PMLA — expanded list","PMLA Schedule covers hundreds of offences as predicate offences: Part A (Indian Penal Code): offences relating to drug trafficking, organised crime. Part B: Offences under NDPS Act. Part C: Offences under foreign exchange, securities, corruption, immigration, narcotics, UAPA, Arms Act, Wildlife Protection Act, POCSO, Cyber laws, Disaster Management Act, Official Secrets Act, Prevention of Corruption Act, Companies Act, customs, and other special Acts. 2023: GST offences (certain frauds) added to Schedule. Any property derived from these scheduled offences is 'proceeds of crime'.","Schedule","Scheduled Offences","Criminal Law / Financial",["PMLA scheduled offences Part A Part B Part C IPC NDPS UAPA Arms Act Wildlife POCSO cyber Companies Act GST customs immigration proceeds crime predicate 2023"]],
// Forest Conservation
["FCA_compensation","Forest Conservation Act — Compensatory afforestation","When forests are diverted for non-forest purposes (under S.2 FCA 1980): compensatory afforestation on equal (or double, in some cases) area of degraded forest/non-forest land is mandatory. Net Present Value (NPV) of the diverted forest must be paid to Compensatory Afforestation Fund Management and Planning Authority (CAMPA). CAMPA Funds used for: afforestation, regeneration, wildlife management. SC in T.N. Godavarman Thirumalpad v. Union of India (1996-ongoing): comprehensive management of forests under supervision.","1","Conservation","Environmental Law / Forest",["FCA compensatory afforestation CAMPA NPV degraded forest non-forest divert mandatory S2 Godavarman SC supervision wildlife management afforestation funds"]],
// Disaster Management
["DMA_NDRF","Disaster Management Act — NDRF and response","S.44-45 DMA: National Disaster Response Force (NDRF) established for disaster response. NDRF: 12 battalions permanently located at strategic locations. SDRF: State Disaster Response Force — each State maintains. S.51 DMA: Any person who fails to comply with or refuses to comply with directions of National/State/District Authority shall be liable to imprisonment up to 1 year or fine or both. During COVID: DMA S.51 was widely used to enforce lockdowns.","4","Response","Disaster Management",["DMA NDRF S44 S45 12 battalions SDRF State S51 fail comply directions imprisonment 1 year fine COVID lockdown disaster response"]],
];
